WHAT: Each event does work proportional to the entire native history accumulated since the epoch, not to the new item: prepare() serializes the whole prepared request once for byte counting, clones the CompletionRequest twice (375, 406), builds the full provider JSON body a second time (the HTTP adapter builds it again when sending), clones chat_history into `input` and compares it message-by-message against accepted_history. record_completed(), tool() and tool_result() each clone `state.turns` (every frame, including every tool result text captured so far), rebuild a NativeHistory and run validate(), which serializes the whole history (`bounded_serialized(self, MAX_NATIVE_PAYLOAD)`) plus every frame again. With N tool calls in a session that is O(N) full-history serializations per call, i.e. O(N^2) bytes serialized over a long native turn; with a few MB of captured results this is tens of ms per tool call, inside rig's hook await (before the tool runs) and on the runner task.

