HOW: Keep running aggregates instead of rebuilding: track total captured bytes incrementally (already done for `state.bytes`), validate only the new frame/result against MAX_TURN_BYTES, and check the payload limit arithmetically (state.bytes + delta <= payload_limit) instead of `from_capture(...)` + `bounded_serialized(&history)`. In prepare(), reuse the single serialization: build the body once and derive the byte count from it, keep `request` by Arc or move it into `pending` instead of cloning twice, and compare only the suffix after `accepted_history.len()` with a stored prefix digest rather than `starts_with` over all messages.

Also (provider-2): Track the serialized byte size incrementally: keep state.bytes (already maintained for tool results) as the running total of the base history + frames, add the size of the new projection/identity/result at the point it is appended (bounded_serialized of just the delta, which record/tool_result already compute for MAX_TURN_BYTES), and compare the running total against payload_limit. Validate structural invariants (from_capture) on the appended frame only, not by rebuilding the whole NativeHistory; build the full history once at persisted_history() time.

VERIFY: Add a unit test that drives NativeCapture with 200 synthetic frames and asserts serialization work is bounded per event (count bytes written through a counting writer per call stays ~ frame size, not history size). Keep src/agent/native_capture.rs tests and the provider_effort_replay_probe tests green; measure wall time of tool_result() at frame 1 vs frame 200.
Also (provider-2): Existing: src/provider_effort_replay_probe.rs native replay tests and src/agent/native_capture tests must stay green. New: a unit test that drives NativeCapture through N tool calls with an instrumented bounded_serialized counter (or a wrapper Write that counts bytes) and asserts the serialized byte volume is O(N) rather than O(N^2); measure one 20-tool-call turn on a 4 MB history before/after.

