WHAT: The canonical tree is hashed BEFORE checking whether any legacy candidate exists, and a marker is only written when a candidate's hash matches. For any install that never had the legacy layout (or whose legacy candidate path differs from the canonical one: canonical sessions are <state>/sessions but candidates are <data>/sessions, memory is <data>/memory vs <config>/agent/memory, etc.) no marker is ever written, so EVERY start re-reads and SHA-256-hashes the entire sessions, tool-outputs, loops and logs directories plus chat_history.json. Verified on this machine: ~/Library/Application Support/zerostack/state has sessions/, tool-outputs/ (3.6 MB), logs/ and chat_history.json but no state/migrations/ directory at all, so this runs in full on each launch. A heavy user with hundreds of MB of sessions/tool outputs pays seconds of synchronous I/O + hashing before the first frame, and the cost grows unboundedly with usage history.

WHERE (main at b56d7a74): src/paths.rs:2394-2421; src/paths.rs:2546-2673; src/paths.rs:2861-2909; src/main.rs:352

HOT PATH: main::run_inner -> paths::converge_legacy_artifacts (main.rs:352, before logging::init and config load, every interactive AND headless -p start) -> migrate_legacy_path x10 artifacts (sessions, tool outputs, loop transcripts, logs, chat history, welcome marker, hook trust, archmd state, memory, skills.db) -> content_identity(canonical) when the marker file is absent. Once per process start, synchronously on the main thread.

