WHAT: For every file, even the overwhelming majority with zero matches, the tool allocates a Vec<&str> with one entry per line (a 1 MiB source file is ~30k entries) and makes one regex call per line. The regex crate's fast paths (memchr/Teddy literal prefilters, lazy DFA over long haystacks) are defeated by per-line invocation overhead (~50-100 ns of setup per call) and the line-splitting pass itself reads the buffer once more. Searching the whole buffer first (`re.is_match(&content)`) rejects non-matching files in one pass, which is how ripgrep achieves its speed; cttyn's review did not touch grep.

